WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.828
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.828 --> 00:00:09.986
I have spent my career so far working and

00:00:09.986 --> 00:00:14.025
living in two very different worlds.

00:00:14.025 --> 00:00:18.089
The first is the world of politics,
where I wrote speeches and

00:00:18.089 --> 00:00:20.835
made ads on two presidential campaigns.

00:00:20.835 --> 00:00:23.629
The second is the world of business,

00:00:23.629 --> 00:00:28.238
where I led special projects
at a major technology company.

00:00:28.238 --> 00:00:34.488
I get asked the exact same
question all the time.

00:00:34.488 --> 00:00:39.942
So tell me, do the politicians and
the CEOs really hate each other as much as

00:00:39.942 --> 00:00:45.672
they look like they do on TV when they're
screaming at each other all the time?

00:00:45.672 --> 00:00:48.436
That question makes me really sad.

00:00:48.436 --> 00:00:53.418
It makes me sad because it says
something about our society that

00:00:53.418 --> 00:00:58.212
everyone just kind of assumes
that our political leaders and

00:00:58.212 --> 00:01:01.606
our business leaders despise each other.

00:01:01.606 --> 00:01:05.421
But you know what makes me even sadder?

00:01:05.421 --> 00:01:10.214
It's actually more true
than I would like to admit.

00:01:10.214 --> 00:01:14.901
Because I've seen firsthand how this
collapsing relationship between our

00:01:14.901 --> 00:01:17.764
political leaders and
our business leaders.

00:01:17.764 --> 00:01:23.265
This lack of decency, this lack of
respect, this lack of fundamental

00:01:23.265 --> 00:01:28.034
communication, is causing
massive problems for society.

00:01:28.034 --> 00:01:29.160
Just look around.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:32.518
It's no secret that things
are pretty screwed up.

00:01:32.518 --> 00:01:35.055
Our planet is literally on fire.

00:01:35.055 --> 00:01:38.655
Our democracies teetering on the edge, and

00:01:38.655 --> 00:01:42.554
people all around us are angry for
good reason.

00:01:42.554 --> 00:01:47.587
We are facing a set of three once
in many generation challenges.

00:01:47.587 --> 00:01:53.078
The first is climate change,
the second is weakening belief

00:01:53.078 --> 00:01:58.259
in democracy, and
the third is widespread inequality.

00:01:58.259 --> 00:02:01.370
And these aren't just government problems,
and

00:02:01.370 --> 00:02:05.675
they're not just business problems,
they are everyone problems.

00:02:05.675 --> 00:02:11.087
And while most of us inherently
understand this, we don't talk about

00:02:11.087 --> 00:02:16.223
the next logical step,
which is to solve these problems, we first

00:02:16.223 --> 00:02:21.742
need to fix the broken relationship
between business and government.

00:02:21.742 --> 00:02:25.196
So what are these problems
that we need to solve?

00:02:25.196 --> 00:02:28.085
I know it's hard talking about
difficult issues like these.

00:02:28.085 --> 00:02:31.283
So I find it's helpful to
talk about specific stories.

00:02:31.283 --> 00:02:34.454
So let's do that for each of these three.

00:02:34.454 --> 00:02:37.435
First, let's talk about the climate.

00:02:37.435 --> 00:02:41.658
For decades, California has been allowed
by the federal government to set

00:02:41.658 --> 00:02:45.221
stricter car emission standards
than the rest of the country.

00:02:45.221 --> 00:02:48.025
But when President Trump came into office,

00:02:48.025 --> 00:02:53.046
he got into a lawsuit with the state of
California to stop this from happening.

00:02:53.046 --> 00:02:57.994
General Motors, along with several other
car makers, signed on to this lawsuit and

00:02:57.994 --> 00:03:01.283
actively encouraged
President Trump to get involved.

00:03:01.283 --> 00:03:08.612
But then, on November 7th 2020,
President Biden won the 2020 election and

00:03:08.612 --> 00:03:14.132
just 16 days later,
General Motors made a big announcement.

00:03:14.132 --> 00:03:18.121
They said they were dropping
their support of the lawsuit and

00:03:18.121 --> 00:03:23.218
were thrilled to work with the new
administration to tackle climate change.

00:03:23.218 --> 00:03:26.034
What does it tell us about our society?

00:03:26.034 --> 00:03:31.285
That one of the biggest companies in
the country changed its mind on one

00:03:31.285 --> 00:03:37.789
of the most important problems facing our
society just because an election happened.

00:03:37.789 --> 00:03:40.676
Ask yourself, is that what bold,

00:03:40.676 --> 00:03:45.070
values-driven corporate
leadership looks like?

00:03:45.070 --> 00:03:51.387
Second, let's talk about the heart
of those elections, our democracy.

00:03:51.387 --> 00:03:55.774
Most of us here have probably
seen political ads online.

00:03:55.774 --> 00:03:58.400
In fact, some of you may even hate them.

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:02.092
In which case I'm really sorry,
some of them are definitely my fault.

00:04:02.092 --> 00:04:04.811
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:04:04.811 --> 00:04:10.168
some of you may have heard of something
called the Federal Election Commission,

00:04:10.168 --> 00:04:15.462
or the FTC, the government agency
responsible for regulating political ads.

00:04:15.462 --> 00:04:21.889
But here's what you may not know,
the last time that the FTC updated its

00:04:21.889 --> 00:04:27.585
regulations on online political
ads was in the year 2006.

00:04:27.585 --> 00:04:32.340
Yes, 2006,
that was back before a tiny company

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:36.555
called Facebook was even
open to the public and

00:04:36.555 --> 00:04:41.330
a full five years before
Snapchat was even founded.

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:46.938
So online political ads right now
are pretty much the Wild West.

00:04:46.938 --> 00:04:52.557
And it's gotten so bad in recent cycles
that the platforms like Google and

00:04:52.557 --> 00:04:54.912
Facebook have had to step in and

00:04:54.912 --> 00:04:59.271
set their own rules because
the FTC was not doing its job.

00:04:59.271 --> 00:05:03.471
Ask yourself,
is that what strong government

00:05:03.471 --> 00:05:07.570
oversight of the business
world looks like?

00:05:07.570 --> 00:05:12.018
Third, let's talk about our
last generational challenge,

00:05:12.018 --> 00:05:13.905
widespread inequality.

00:05:13.905 --> 00:05:17.658
A few years ago,
DoorDash had an unusual tipping policy.

00:05:17.658 --> 00:05:21.537
Dashers would go out and deliver food and
DoorDash would guarantee

00:05:21.537 --> 00:05:25.484
them a minimum payment to ensure
that if a customer did not tip them,

00:05:25.484 --> 00:05:28.200
they would still bring
a certain amount home.

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:33.372
That's a great idea, but
if a customer did tip them, DoorDash

00:05:33.372 --> 00:05:39.538
would lower their payment to the dasher
by the exact same amount as the tip.

00:05:39.538 --> 00:05:43.498
So DoorDash was basically like,
thank you very much for the money, and

00:05:43.498 --> 00:05:44.693
put it in its pocket.

00:05:46.887 --> 00:05:50.151
There was a huge uproar
when this came out.

00:05:50.151 --> 00:05:54.217
The CEO apologized,
the policy was changed.

00:05:54.217 --> 00:05:58.434
And while nothing indicates that
this was purposely malicious,

00:05:58.434 --> 00:06:03.189
ask yourself, where were the government
regulators who should have been

00:06:03.189 --> 00:06:08.265
making rules to ensure that everyone in
the gig economy could earn a fair wage?

00:06:08.265 --> 00:06:13.252
And where were the corporate
executives who could have spoken to

00:06:13.252 --> 00:06:18.347
Dashers to understand how this
policy would have impacted them?

00:06:18.347 --> 00:06:22.324
Now that we've talked
through these three examples,

00:06:22.324 --> 00:06:26.906
I would bet there's at least one
person in this audience who's

00:06:26.906 --> 00:06:31.668
thinking something like this,
geez, this guy's kind of naive.

00:06:31.668 --> 00:06:33.288
What does he expect?

00:06:33.288 --> 00:06:35.369
That all these folks in
the political world and

00:06:35.369 --> 00:06:38.312
all these folks in the business
world are just going to get along?

00:06:38.312 --> 00:06:39.758
Come on, live in reality.

00:06:39.758 --> 00:06:43.113
They have such opposing interests.

00:06:43.113 --> 00:06:44.571
And that's a fair point.

00:06:44.571 --> 00:06:46.989
Believe me, I've heard it before.

00:06:46.989 --> 00:06:50.217
But here is what that argument misses.

00:06:50.217 --> 00:06:55.435
Our political leaders and
our business leaders have one very

00:06:55.435 --> 00:07:00.458
common interest, and
that's keeping a stable society.

00:07:00.458 --> 00:07:05.082
And a stable society
includes a stable planet,

00:07:05.082 --> 00:07:09.252
a stable democracy, and a stable people.

00:07:09.252 --> 00:07:14.884
Because here is a fundamental truth,
you cannot make money and

00:07:14.884 --> 00:07:22.122
you cannot govern, in the long run,
when everything around you is going crazy.

00:07:22.122 --> 00:07:24.567
That's why these aren't just
government problems and

00:07:24.567 --> 00:07:26.290
they're not just business problems.

00:07:26.290 --> 00:07:31.296
They are everyone problems and
it is naive, incredibly,

00:07:31.296 --> 00:07:38.345
ridiculously, laughably naive, to believe
that we can keep a stable society for

00:07:38.345 --> 00:07:43.044
decades into the future when
our political leaders and

00:07:43.044 --> 00:07:49.092
our business leaders cannot even
approach each other with goodwill.

00:07:51.359 --> 00:07:55.112
But here's the good news,
things are changing.

00:07:55.112 --> 00:07:56.400
Rebecca Henderson,

00:07:56.400 --> 00:08:00.904
a professor at Harvard Business School,
has been writing over the past few

00:08:00.904 --> 00:08:05.347
years about the need to re-imagine
capitalism to fight climate change.

00:08:05.347 --> 00:08:09.634
And Larry Fink, the leader of one of
the world's largest asset managers,

00:08:09.634 --> 00:08:14.684
has been writing, recently, about the need
for companies to find profit and purpose.

00:08:14.684 --> 00:08:17.619
But we need all of our
leaders to take action.

00:08:17.619 --> 00:08:20.925
And that's where everyone
here today comes in.

00:08:20.925 --> 00:08:25.941
Because remember both of those
companies that I just talked about,

00:08:25.941 --> 00:08:31.838
General Motors and DoorDash, well,
their CEOs are both Stamford MBA alumni.

00:08:31.838 --> 00:08:35.426
So that means in ten years,

00:08:35.426 --> 00:08:40.269
maybe even five, you could be them.

00:08:40.269 --> 00:08:42.799
So here's what we need to do.

00:08:42.799 --> 00:08:49.498
First, for all the business leaders here
today, before making a big decision,

00:08:49.498 --> 00:08:54.424
ask yourself this question,
in ten years from now will this

00:08:54.424 --> 00:08:58.976
decision make society a better place or
a worse place?

00:08:58.976 --> 00:09:03.898
You might be surprised how many
decisions have a clear-cut

00:09:03.898 --> 00:09:07.053
answer to that very simple question.

00:09:07.053 --> 00:09:13.878
Second, for all the political leaders
here, before making a big decision,

00:09:13.878 --> 00:09:18.498
ask yourself this question,
in ten years from now,

00:09:18.498 --> 00:09:25.334
will this decision help those companies
make the right choices for society?

00:09:25.334 --> 00:09:30.026
Because your job is to set the market's
rules to incentivize those

00:09:30.026 --> 00:09:34.887
businesses to make good decisions
not just for their shareholders,

00:09:34.887 --> 00:09:38.602
but for all of us and
especially your constituents.

00:09:38.602 --> 00:09:43.566
Third, for all the students here today,
the future leaders,

00:09:43.566 --> 00:09:49.576
there is a change coming to the way
business interacts with the wider world.

00:09:49.576 --> 00:09:54.301
If you listen closely
you can hear it coming.

00:09:54.301 --> 00:09:58.168
Think deeply about the role you
have to play in that movement.

00:09:58.168 --> 00:10:01.241
Think about the companies you join and

00:10:01.241 --> 00:10:05.960
the projects you take on,
because they really do matter.

00:10:05.960 --> 00:10:08.569
Fourth, and this is for everyone here,

00:10:08.569 --> 00:10:11.497
do not wait for
someone else to take the lead.

00:10:11.497 --> 00:10:13.716
We do not have the time to waste.

00:10:13.716 --> 00:10:16.425
We all need to get started on this today.

00:10:16.425 --> 00:10:19.287
Because remember,
these aren't just government problems and

00:10:19.287 --> 00:10:21.040
they're not just business problems.

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:25.558
They are everyone problems, and
we need to work together to solve them.

00:10:27.314 --> 00:10:32.391
So if you're sitting here today and you're
already worried about what the planet

00:10:32.391 --> 00:10:37.099
is going to look like for your kids, or
you can't even imagine what's going to

00:10:37.099 --> 00:10:41.810
happen in the next election, or you walk
down the street and are horrified by

00:10:41.810 --> 00:10:46.838
the inequality you see all around you,
you already know how high the stakes are.

00:10:50.862 --> 00:10:54.904
So what are you going to do about it?

00:10:54.904 --> 00:10:56.607
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:10:56.607 --> 00:11:01.709
[MUSIC]

